From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:30:00 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 20/20] feat(editor): multi-level numbering, Persian letters and Abjad, numbers styled like the text
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

VERSION: 1.2 (2026-10-16)  1.2: an item joined to a list after other content (e.g. as
  5) and then moved to the next level with Tab was numbered 4.5 instead of 4.1: a later
  segment used its stored start number (kept for Markdown) to start the new level. Only
  the first segment of a list, and nested lists, use their own start number now.
  1.1: content between two segments of the same list (the
  text of an item, e.g. paragraphs after "الف)" before "ب)") is indented to where the
  text of the last item before it starts, at that item's level.
  1.0: first version.
Root: build/outline-1.11.0 (apply after 0001..0019)

Numbers are computed, not stored (in the editor):
- Every numbered list (segment) may carry a list id; segments with the same
  id are numbered as one list, in document order, also when other content
  lies between them. Inserting, deleting or moving an item renumbers the
  rest. Lists without an id behave as before (each starts at its start).
- A segment has a level (0 = first); a list nested in an item is one level
  deeper. Numbers per level restart under each item of the level above.
- The number is drawn in the margin of the item's first line with the font,
  size, weight, slant and color of the item's text (a heading in a list item
  gives its number the heading's size and weight). The list marker is hidden.
  HTML without the editor and Markdown keep normal list numbers; later
  segments keep their start number in `order` for them.

Menu "Numbering" (formatting toolbar, replaces "Number style"):
- Number type of the level: default, 1 2 3 (Persian digits in RTL lists),
  Persian alphabet الف ب پ, Abjad الف ب ج د, a b c, A B C. Set on one item, it
  applies to every item of that level of the list.
- Suffix . ) - for the whole list.
- Full number 1.2.1 (on/off) for the whole list; mixed types give e.g. ۱.الف
- Number color per level (or the color of the text): e.g. level 1 green,
  level 2 red, for all items of the level.
- Next level / Previous level (also Tab / Shift+Tab, Mod+] / Mod+[).
- Select all items of this level; Join previous numbering; Restart
  numbering (from 1).
- A paragraph after a numbered list (also with other content between) shows
  the menu with "Join previous numbering" only.

Text of an item (1.1):
- Blocks that lie between two segments of the same list belong to the last
  item before them: they start where that item's text starts (its level).
  Content after the last segment of a list is not indented.

Levels:
- Tab nests an item under the one before; the first item of a segment (e.g.
  a paragraph just joined after other content) gets a segment of its own one
  level deeper, so it becomes 2.1 and the next items of level 1 go on.
- Shift+Tab moves back one level; on the first level the item leaves the
  list as before. Neighbouring segments of the same list and level are
  joined again.

Selecting (with patch 0017):
- A click on a number selects all items of its level in the list (their
  first lines are picked parts, the last one the selection); Ctrl/Cmd+click
  adds or removes one item. Tab / Shift+Tab, the menu, and formatting then
  act on all picked items; the parts stay picked.
- Numbering several parts picked with Ctrl gives them one list id.
- Pasted lists get new ids (a pasted copy is numbered on its own).

Files (9; N = new, M = modified, D = deleted):
